  <h2>What to look for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The 2020–21 shelf.</strong> In every 2020 and 2021 vintage, all dots for the current year sat at 0.125% — the zero lower bound, a flat shelf at the bottom of the cloud.</li>
    <li><strong>The 2022–23 hiking wall.</strong> From early 2022 the cloud climbs steeply, from near zero to above 5% — the fastest tightening in the sample.</li>
    <li><strong>The outliers are real.</strong> September 2015 holds the only sub-zero dots in the sample (one at −0.125% for end-2015 and one for end-2016). June and September 2023 each hold one dot at 6.125%, the highest rate in the dataset.</li>
    <li><strong>The longer-run drift.</strong> The neutral-rate median in the panel above fell from 4.25% in 2012 to 3.25% in September 2026.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this is — and what it isn't</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
    A dot is one FOMC participant's view of the <em>appropriate</em>
    federal funds rate for a given year, under their own economic outlook.
    It is not a forecast of what the Committee will do, and the dots are
    anonymous — no dot can be tied to a person, and no person's dots can
    be followed across vintages. This page shows the distribution moving
    over time, never an individual's path, and it draws no
    prediction-accuracy line: that would need a different dataset and a
    different argument.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
    Dots are the Fed's published Figure 2 table values, rounded to the
    nearest 1/8 percentage point (1/4 point in 2012–2013). Medians before
    September 2016 are derived from the dots, because Table 1 is not in
    the accessible HTML for those vintages — they are labelled derived in
    the chart's hover text, never presented as published Fed values.
    Participant counts run 14–19 per vintage-year and vary for real
    reasons (vacant seats); in 2026 only 18 participants submitted, as
    Chair Warsh did not submit projections. Marker size encodes how many
    participants sit at one rate — there is no random jitter, so every
    marker sits exactly on its published coordinate. The next SEP is
    expected with the December 8–9, 2026 meeting.
  </p>
  <p>
    Source: <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcprojtabl20260916.htm">Federal Reserve — FOMC Summary of Economic Projections, projection materials</a> (Figure 2 data tables + Table 1), retrieved Nov. 20, 2026. Next SEP expected Dec. 9, 2026 — see the Fed's <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm">meeting calendars</a>.
  </p>
